{"data":{"attachmentkeynames":["rs"],"desc":"\"Master moved - bring my branch up to date.\" fetch --prune -> RESTORE FIRST: a library pushed here from a peer (dev.git.overlay) is discarded, its files put back to the branch's HEAD -> merge origin/<default> (master, else main; the local default when there is no origin) into the current branch -> dev.git.after_update (the install step: apps the merged libraries ship are refreshed into runtime/ - never activated - and libraries whose store changed are rebuilt) -> the restored libraries are re-installed (apps refreshed, rebuilt) even where the merge did not touch them, and even when nothing was merged -> push when the branch tracks a remote. The verb pull cannot do this: it targets the branch's own upstream, which for a solo developer never moves. Requires that the repo is ON `branch`, no conflicts, no merge in flight; a fetch failure merges nothing. A dirty tree is not a blocker: git refuses a merge that would overwrite an edit (reported as refused, nothing merged) and untouched edits ride along, reported as `carried`. Already-current is an ok no-op (merged 0) apart from the restore. Refuses the default branch itself - a test peer sitting on master goes back to git with dev.github.update. A real conflict is reported with the tree left mid-merge on the branch - resolve then commit, or merge --abort - never half-merged. Returns {status, msg, branch, base, merged (commit count), restored (peer-pushed libraries discarded), pushed, push_err, carried, restart (a static library's Rust changed), install (after_update's result), steps}.","import":"use ndata::dataarray::DataArray;","params":[{"name":"repo","type":"String"},{"name":"branch","type":"String"}],"returntype":"FLAT","type":"rust"},"id":"wrwzmq1a0733d8548hb","readers":[],"time":1795224109584,"username":"system","writers":[]}